{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Sarah conceived and bore Abraham a son.</b> The verse says ״וַתַּהַר״ — this may be explained along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 53:5): she had no womb (lit. 'hostel'), therefore Scripture informs us that there was a miracle even in the conception. And the verse's saying <b>to Abraham</b> alludes that this son was to Abraham, and not Ishmael to Abraham — for he would not be called Abraham's son, as it is written (Genesis 21:12): ״כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זָרַע״. And although we find that Scripture said (Genesis 16:15): ״וַתֵּלֶד הָגָר לְאַבְרָם בֵּן״ — there he was still called Abram; but after the name Abram was uprooted, so that this name would no longer be remembered of him but rather Abraham, Ishmael has no claim to be called Abraham's son. And perhaps this is the reason: one who calls Abraham 'Abram' transgresses a positive commandment (Berakhot 13a); the reason for HaShem's blessed insistence is that when he is called Abram, one traces Ishmael to him as a son, and HaShem said: ״כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זָרַע״ — and this can only be when the name Abram is uprooted. And this reasoning does not apply to Jacob; therefore even though he was named Israel, there is no insistence that he not be called Jacob. And see what I wrote on the verse (Genesis 35:10) ״לֹא יִקָּרֵא״ etc., and you will find another reason:" "<b>For his old age.</b> The reason for the informing, and also the verse's saying <b>for his old age</b> rather than 'for his old age' (לְזִקְנָתוֹ) — according to what we have written, that at the time of the birth there was lacking a soul from the supernal world for procreation, and he had only [a soul] from the feminine world (מֵעָלְמָא דְּנוּקְבָּא). Scripture intends to say that she gave birth then, and also gave birth in him the preparation for another, second 'old age' of Abraham — for you must know that it is a fundamental principle that a person cannot attain anything new in this world unless the preparation of the receiver precedes, as is known to the masters of truth. And if Yitzchak had possessed only the soul from the feminine world (מֵעָלְמָא דְּנוּקְבָּא) and had been lacking the preparation for receiving a soul from the masculine world (מֵעָלְמָא דִּדְכוּרָא), the Binding (עֲקֵדָה) would not have availed him. And this is what Scripture precisioned by saying <b>and she bore</b> etc. — it informed that she gave birth for two periods of his old age: the first is the time of his birth, and the second is a preparation for another old age of Abraham, which is the time of the Binding (עֲקֵדָה)." "Or it may allude by <b>for his old age</b> — his old age and his wife's old age, along the lines of what she said: ״אַחֲרֵי בְלוֹתִי״ etc. ״וַאדֹנִי זָקֵן״ (Genesis 18:12)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:44.544074821Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}